{
  "dom_id": "step-q112241",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/e16ae124ce49bff01ffac74a56d29848d02afd650cb5e230a1ea3ef2ff0e7aa7.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}f(x)-12?$",
  "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=\\frac{3}{2}f(x)-12,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{3}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}f(x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/5707b8c5cdf4d91b3fcc2aba75242af7c8df31c8e541c4a0e06f22d8493b27cc.png)\n\n- Then, translate it down by $12$ units to get the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}f(x)-12.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/2670e9453ba10db44d72ca5a1c68c2806b9668cc3867c692f7e233a88ce98043.png)",
